.TH DIDATE 8 "2026-10-26"
.SH NAME
didate \- convert dates and Diablo time values
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B didate
.I datestr
.br
.B didate
.B \-c
.I unixtime
.br
.B didate
.B \-g
.I histime
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I didate
prints a date in
.IR ctime (3)
form.  Given a date string, it parses it the way diablo parses dates
(for example a Date: header or "26 Oct 2026 12:00:00 GMT") and prints
the result, or "Illegal format".
.PP
The dates it reads, and so the Date: headers diablo can check against
feedermaxacceptage: RFC 5322/822 ("Mon, 01 Jan 2001 12:00:00 +0000", with
or without weekday and seconds, two, three or four digit years), RFC 850
("Monday, 01-Jan-01 12:00:00 GMT"), ctime and date(1)
("Mon Jan  1 12:00:00 UTC 2001"), US dates ("Jan 1, 2001 1:30 PM"),
ISO 8601 ("2001-01-01T12:00:00Z", "20010101T120000Z"), "01.01.2001",
and Unix time.  The time zone is applied (a numeric offset, UT, UTC, GMT,
Z, EST/EDT/CST/CDT/MST/MDT/PST/PDT and a few European and Asian names;
any other name counts as UTC).  The month is English (any word that
starts with the three letter abbreviation) or a common German, French,
Spanish, Italian or Dutch one.  A date with the day and month in either
order (01/02/2001) or without a year is not read.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-c " unixtime"
Print the date of a Unix time (seconds since 1970), decimal or 0x hex.
.TP
.BI \-g " histime"
Print the date of a history time value (minutes since 1970, the gm=
field shown by
.IR dilookup (8)),
decimal or 0x hex.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
didate "Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:00:00 GMT"
didate -c 1793039519
didate -g 29883850
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
dilookup(8),
didump(8),
diablo-files(5)
